WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.611 --> 00:00:04.003
-Our next guest is a talented
actor, writer, and director

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.506
you know from "Broad City"
and the film "Rough Night."

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.208
He is the co-creator,
co-showrunner,

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:11.244
and one of the stars of "Hacks,"
which is streaming on HBO Max.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.612
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.180
-Hey, look, if you're just
calling to shoot the [bleep]

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.115
love you, bless you,
but I'll call you back?

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:17.650
-Alrighty. Ciao.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.385
-Okay, ciao for now.

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:20.686
-Here you go, Prince.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:22.255
-Thank you so much.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.056
-Yum.

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:25.625
-Okay, not "yum."

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.927
Weird and bizarre.
What is in this one?

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.095
-Honey.
-This isn't --

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.963
-You said natural.
It comes from bears.

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:32.632
-Ooh! Kayla...

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:36.235
-Please welcome to the show
Paul W. Downs, everybody.

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:39.405
♪♪

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.107
-Oh.

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:46.179
I, um -- I'm so sorry.
-Well, okay.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.015
Yeah, um...

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:50.250
Oh. Okay.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.018
-So...

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.319
-Yeah, I'm so sorry.
-Okay.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:56.623
-I thought this was a Zoom.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.057
-Oh!
-Yes.

00:00:58.057 --> 00:00:59.892
I know they said "in studio,"
but I thought

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:01.261
the Wi-Fi was better.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.530
I thought it was
going to be all virtual, so...

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.764
-So you thought
you would be staying

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:07.333
in the dressing room
on a laptop.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.668
-Well, yeah.
Nicole and Jake are not here.

00:01:09.668 --> 00:01:10.803
-Right.
-Okay.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.005
I thought they were
also doing that.

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:16.108
I thought we were all here and
we were just Zooming.

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:20.780
-Well, I mean, at least you
wore very clean socks.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.648
-Thank you, yes.
No holes in the socks.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:25.318
Yeah, oops. Well...

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.753
-Now, it is nice
to have you here.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:29.055
-Thank you.
It's very nice to be here.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:30.289
-You've been here before.
You've been in this studio.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:31.357
Well, not in this studio,

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.558
but you've been in
30 Rock before.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:34.760
-I've been in
30 Rock before, yep.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.496
It's a trigger, you know?

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.064
-It is triggering for you.
-It can be.

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:39.265
-Okay.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:43.636
-I screen-tested for
"SNL" when I was like 23.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.672
And it was a nerve-racking day.
-It is very nerve-racking.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:48.607
-First of all, I mean,
the whole thing is wild.

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:53.412
But when I first got here, I
used my card to pay for the cab.

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:56.850
Declined. I think I had negative
dollars in my bank account.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.384
Thank God my partner,
Lucia Aniello,

00:01:58.384 --> 00:01:59.785
was dropping me off,
so she paid.

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:03.222
So I owe the screen test to her,
as I owe many things to her.

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:08.460
And, yet, you know, I never like
to use public restrooms.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.830
You know, I like to, like,
be in my place...

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.565
-I agree with you 100%.
-...set the mood,

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:15.335
light a candle, you know,
create a vibe.

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:18.671
But a producer came to me
and knowingly said,

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.074
"If you need a bathroom,
it is right down there."

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:24.644
And I had a nervous tummy,
you know, as one does.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:28.280
And so I used it.
I used that bathroom.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.515
And then I screen-tested.

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.017
And I think you may
have been there.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.019
-I think I probably was, based
on that timeline, yeah.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.788
-You probably remember.
I mean, I killed it.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:36.990
-Did great.
-Incredibly well.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.090
It went well.
-So well.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.625
-Laughter, applau--
I think you stood.

00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:40.793
I think you stood,
you clapped, you cheered.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.261
-And I never stand.
-That's the thing.

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:44.830
I was like,
"This is in the bag."

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.567
And then, about a week later,
I found out I did not get it.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:52.371
And it occurred to me
it was probably a janitor

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:55.808
who had Lorne's ear and said,

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:56.943
"The building can't handle it.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.477
The pipes are old.

00:02:58.477 --> 00:03:00.746
We cannot have him
in the building, okay?

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:01.781
He will destroy it.
30 Rock will go."

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:02.949
-He marched in.
-Yeah.

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:05.418
-We were about --
I mean, your name was about

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:08.621
to go, and then he marched in
and said, "It's him or me."

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.490
-Hey, you know what? Better him.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.591
Better him.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.325
We'd be in New Jersey
filming this.

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:15.261
This building would be totaled.

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:16.729
-I am so happy, in the end,

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.063
that it has worked out
so well for you.

00:03:18.063 --> 00:03:20.066
This is such a fantastic show.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:24.137
-And I want to first ask
what it feels like

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:26.405
when you write
a character this good

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:30.910
and then you get an actor as
good as Jean Smart who says yes.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.079
I mean, it just must
be so rewarding as a writer

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:35.548
to have that come through.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.450
-Yeah, it was a dream come true.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.251
It was wild.
We couldn't believe it.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:42.921
You know, when we wrote
the show, we made this character

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:45.124
an amalgamation of, like,
Lucille Ball, Phyllis Diller,

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.625
Joan Rivers, Debbie Reynolds,

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:47.827
a lot of, like,
show-biz veterans.

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.562
And, so, we didn't
have anyone in mind,

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:52.131
but because the show
is really grounded

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.700
and has, you know,
moments that are

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.536
more dramatic and emotional,
we wanted someone that could

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:00.673
play the comedy
and be a believable stand-up

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:04.009
but also do those sort of more
heartfelt, dramatic moments,

00:04:04.009 --> 00:04:07.213
as well, and, honestly,
the list is not very long

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:09.448
of women who can
do that in equal measure.

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:11.050
And, for us, Jean Smart
was at the top of it.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.118
So we were thrilled.

00:04:13.118 --> 00:04:17.189
-This is an idea you've had
for a very long time.

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:18.724
Explain where you
came up with it

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:20.292
and what it feels
like to see it to fruition

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:21.627
all these years later.

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:27.366
-Yeah, well, we were on
a road trip to Monster Jam,

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:31.303
monster-truck rally, myself and
Jen Statsky and Lucia Aniello,

00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:33.338
my co-creators
and co-conspirators.

00:04:33.338 --> 00:04:36.508
And, as one does -- When we're
together, we're always

00:04:36.508 --> 00:04:38.845
pitching and we're always
talking about bits and ideas.

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.312
-Do you go to --

00:04:40.312 --> 00:04:42.381
Was this your first Monster Jam
or have you been before?

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:44.050
-It was not.
-Okay.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.851
-Yes.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:47.720
I was filming
my Netflix special

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.025
and I was doing a character
named Jasper Cooch.

00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:54.026
You may famously know
Jasper Cooch.

00:04:54.026 --> 00:04:57.230
He's famously, "Big Trucks!"
That's pretty much it.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.664
-Gotcha.
-That's the character.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:02.001
And, so, yeah, we were going to
Monster Jam to film this.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:06.506
And we started talking about
a lot of female comedians

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:08.074
who we didn't feel like
got the same

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:10.009
acclaim and, you know, credit

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:11.711
that a lot of
their male counterparts had.

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:14.713
And, so, yeah,
this sort of fabulous

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:16.115
female comedian character was

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.284
born of a
Monster Jam truck rally.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:19.518
-That's really nice.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:22.555
-And here it is -- So, you --
From the clip,

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:26.224
you also get to play
the agent in this show

00:05:26.224 --> 00:05:29.528
that connects both Jean Smart
with a younger writer,

00:05:29.528 --> 00:05:31.130
someone to freshen up her act.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:35.034
What did you want to
bring to the role of agent?

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:37.436
Because, obviously,
you know, this isn't --

00:05:37.436 --> 00:05:39.738
You're not the first person
to play an agent on television,

00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:42.174
but it's sort of
a unique bounce.

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:45.244
-Well, you know,
I believe it's really important

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.379
for there to be representation
for representation.

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:48.648
-Sure, sure.
-You know?

00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:50.516
I really think, if you can
see it, you can be it.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.484
And I think, you know,
for young people,

00:05:52.484 --> 00:05:53.886
to be in the industry
is a dream,

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.288
and I want to show them
it can happen.

00:05:56.288 --> 00:05:58.390
But, yeah, I hope that this
character is a little different

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.026
than ones you may have
seen before,

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:02.261
because he honestly has
good intentions.

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:04.931
He's not the slick, slimy
agent or manager.

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:07.099
He's somebody who really has

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:11.036
the best of
his client's needs at heart.

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:13.272
So, they really do
need each other,

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:15.341
and so, in the end, I think
he did a good job.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:17.643
I think he's a good guy.

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.145
-While you have not been
an agent or manager,

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:20.846
you have had some gigs
over the years

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:23.248
where you gotten the numbers,
the phone numbers,

00:06:23.248 --> 00:06:24.617
of very famous people.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:25.851
-Yes.

00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:28.087
-Or I should say,
maybe, they have gotten yours.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.056
-Both. You know what? Both.

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.191
Excuse me.
I just have to adjust.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.759
[ Clears throat ]

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:35.595
It's cold in here.

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:37.963
Yeah, we -- I actually --
My first day job,

00:06:37.963 --> 00:06:40.899
I worked for this entertainment,
marketing, and PR company.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.268
And there was an event,
and Goldie Hawn

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:44.737
was coming in for it.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.973
And they said, "You know,
why don't you sort of

00:06:47.973 --> 00:06:49.141
be her person.

00:06:49.141 --> 00:06:50.643
If she needs anything,
you're her person."

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:52.111
So I hung around with
her that night.

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.646
And she actually was like,
"You know what?

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.849
Can I take your phone number
in case I need your help?"

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.085
So I gave her my phone number.

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:00.085
And that was that.

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:02.455
And then,
about five years later,

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:04.256
I was with some friends

00:07:04.256 --> 00:07:08.060
and I get a phone call from
Goldie Hawn.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.596
And I was like,
"Guys, I'm sorry.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.865
Goldie Hawn is calling me.
I got to go take this."

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:14.766
So, I pick up.

00:07:14.766 --> 00:07:18.237
And she says,
"Paul, baby, it's Goldie."

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:21.473
And I was like, "Hi, Goldie."
"How are you, baby?"

00:07:21.473 --> 00:07:23.475
"I'm good. How are you?"

00:07:23.475 --> 00:07:25.644
"Who are you?"

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:28.180
"Um...excuse me?
You just called me.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.449
It's Paul."

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.784
"Yeah, but Paul who?"
"Paul Downs."

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.218
"Not ringing a bell, baby."

00:07:33.218 --> 00:07:36.088
And I was like, "Okay, we met at
this event like five years ago."

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:38.357
And she was like, "Oh!
Okay, great.

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.259
Kurt and I were just
going through our phone,

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:43.429
and we didn't know who you were,
but thanks so much.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:45.598
Bye."

00:07:45.598 --> 00:07:47.800
Yeah. And I think
it was "delete."

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.268
-Yeah.

00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:52.237
-But I still have her number,
so, you know, if I need it...

00:07:52.237 --> 00:07:53.906
-In case of an emergency,

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:56.175
you could get ahold of her
if need be.

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:57.343
-Yes.

00:07:57.343 --> 00:08:00.179
-I'm going to make you
say or tell us

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:02.048
one last thing before you go,
'cause it's --

00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:04.584
It delights me to no end.
-Oh, no.

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:06.618
-When you first
saw the show "Seinfeld,"

00:08:06.618 --> 00:08:08.887
it was framed
differently for you.

00:08:08.887 --> 00:08:10.323
-Yeah. Well -- Yeah.

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:12.724
I -- It was probably like
seventh grade,

00:08:12.724 --> 00:08:15.694
and this girl, Courtney --
I was very gullible.

00:08:15.694 --> 00:08:20.899
And this girl, Courtney, told me
that "Seinfeld" was a show about

00:08:20.899 --> 00:08:26.205
a man living with AIDS,
and I believed her.

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:31.477
And so I watched "Seinfeld"
thinking Jerry had AIDS.

00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:33.111
And I got to tell you,
it's good, okay?

00:08:33.111 --> 00:08:35.147
It's very profound.
-Really? Okay.

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:37.349
It doesn't hurt it.
-It's absolutely premium.

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:39.685
I was like,
"They don't even mention it?

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:42.120
That's slick, okay?
This is good writing.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.289
It should be --"
No offense to NBC,

00:08:44.289 --> 00:08:46.792
but I'm like, "This is on HBO,
this is wild."

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:48.060
It had a whole new meaning.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.228
You know, Kramer busts in,
eats his cereal,

00:08:50.228 --> 00:08:52.031
you're like, "Come on.

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:53.599
This guy's got AIDS, you know?

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:54.833
Knock."

00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:56.201
Jerry kind find a partner,
you're like,

00:08:56.201 --> 00:08:58.237
"Well...he's living with AIDS."

00:08:58.237 --> 00:08:59.605
-Wow!

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:03.008
-Yeah, whole new perspective and
context for the Soup Nazi.

00:09:03.008 --> 00:09:05.010
I mean, that guy's a bigot.
You know what I mean?

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.580
-Yeah. He was already a bad guy.
-Try it.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.615
Watch one episode,
and it really does work.

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.950
-Well, that's --
I mean, I love the show as is,

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:14.620
but I could have a whole nother
way to watch it.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.388
-Well, if it's not about a man
living with AIDS,

00:09:16.388 --> 00:09:17.890
what is it about?

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:21.393
Nothing? You know?
I mean...that's...

00:09:21.393 --> 00:09:24.596
-"Hacks"
is streaming on HBO Max.

00:09:24.596 --> 00:09:25.931
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:25.931 --> 00:09:28.266
And I'm so sorry about
the miscommunication on Zoom.

00:09:28.266 --> 00:09:30.969
-I'm very sorry.
It's my bad, so...

00:09:30.969 --> 00:09:32.038
-We'll be right back.

